---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CardGrid from '../../../components/CardGrid.astro';
import DocCard from '../../../components/DocCard.astro';
const sidebar = [
  { title: 'Documentation Home', href: '/docs' },
  { title: 'Observability Overview', href: '/docs/observability' },
  { title: 'SIEM & Log Pipeline', href: '/docs/observability/siem', active: true },
  { title: 'Kibana Dashboards', href: '/docs/observability/kibana' },
];
const toc = [
  { id: 'timeline', text: 'Timeline Visualization', level: 2 },
  { id: 'dataflow', text: 'Data Flow', level: 2 },
  { id: 'transport', text: 'Transport & mTLS', level: 2 },
  { id: 'contracts', text: 'Pipeline Contracts', level: 2 },
  { id: 'access-log', text: 'Security Access Log', level: 2 },
  { id: 'pillars', text: 'Logs, Metrics and Traces', level: 2 },
];
---
<DocsLayout title="SIEM & Log Pipeline" description="How logs are processed and analyzed using SIEM tools" section="Observability" sidebar={sidebar} toc={toc}>
  <p>
    The SIEM path is intentionally one-directional. The edge writes structured events and ships them out over
    mTLS; the SIEM host indexes and visualises them. Nothing in the request path waits for Elasticsearch or
    Kibana to answer.
  </p>
  <CardGrid columns={3}>
    <DocCard title="One direction" icon="route" tag="Outbound only" description="The edge opens the connection and ships; the SIEM never connects into the edge network." meta="vector_outbound → vector_inbound" />
    <DocCard title="Mutual TLS" icon="lock" tag="Transport" description="A private CA signs both ends; the agent certificate is copied to the edge before shipping starts." meta="gen_elk_certs.sh" />
    <DocCard title="Typed fields" icon="search" tag="VRL enrich" description="Security lines are parsed into real fields so dashboards can aggregate on route, score and decay state." meta="per-type daily indices" />
  </CardGrid>

  <h2 id="dataflow">Data Flow</h2>
  <pre><code>reverse_proxy (Lua)  → SECURITY_EVENT lines in the Nginx error log
                     → security access log (decision_trace fields)
Suricata             → eve.json
        ↓ edge Vector shipper (vector_outbound), tags log_type
        ↓ mTLS
SIEM Vector aggregator (vector_inbound) → VRL enrich → Elasticsearch honeypot-&#123;log_type&#125;-YYYY.MM.DD
        ↓
Kibana dashboards (honeypot-* data view)</code></pre>
  <p>The request-side pipeline that produces these events is in
  <a href="/docs/architecture#pipeline">Architecture → Request Pipeline</a>. To check that the hop is actually
  delivering, use the dashboard's <a href="/docs/getting-started/dashboard#monitor">Log Pipeline</a> page.</p>
  <h2 id="transport">Transport & mTLS</h2>
  <p>The SIEM host (<code>siem/</code>) and the honeypot host (<code>ids/</code>) are a two-host split — see
  <a href="/docs/architecture">Architecture</a> for why. The Vector hop between them is secured with mutual TLS:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>siem/certs/root-ca/gen_elk_certs.sh</code> creates a CA and signs certificates for <code>es01</code>,
    <code>kibana</code>, <code>vector</code> and <code>vector-agent</code>. The <code>vector-agent</code> client
    certificate is copied to the honeypot host's <code>ids/vector/certs/</code> before its shipper can connect.</li>
    <li>The <code>vector</code> server certificate carries a Subject Alternative Name list so the agent can connect
    by hostname (<code>host.docker.internal</code> for single-host testing) or by raw IP (<code>VECTOR_HOST</code>
    in a real two-host deployment).</li>
    <li>Vector's Elasticsearch sink writes to <code>honeypot-&#123;log_type&#125;-%Y.%m.%d</code>, using the
    <code>log_type</code> field the edge shipper tags on every event, so each log type and day lands in its own index.</li>
    <li>A VRL <code>enrich</code> transform in <code>vector/vector.yaml</code> parses the <code>nginx_security</code>
    lines into real, typed fields (<code>route</code>, <code>risk_score</code>, <code>suspicious</code>, the decision
    fields above) instead of one opaque <code>message</code> string.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="admonition note">
    <div class="admonition-title">The edge never blocks on the SIEM</div>
    <p>Vector buffers locally and the sink retries, so an Elasticsearch or Kibana outage shows up as ingestion lag,
    never as a failed or slowed request in the honeypot's path.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="contracts">Pipeline Contracts</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Events are JSON lines.</strong> <code>log_security_event()</code> emits a single JSON object per line, prefixed so Vector can filter without parsing unrelated Nginx errors.</li>
    <li><strong>Arrays are arrays.</strong> Empty Lua tables are tagged with <code>cjson.empty_array_mt</code>, so they encode as <code>[]</code> instead of <code>{}</code> and do not trigger Elasticsearch mapping conflicts.</li>
    <li><strong>Retries never block the edge.</strong> Vector buffers locally; a SIEM outage shows up as lag, not as failed requests.</li>
    <li><strong>Dashboards are code.</strong> Saved objects are generated from Python and committed as NDJSON, which makes reviews and rollbacks possible.</li>
    <li><strong>Exports preserve manual work.</strong> If a panel was edited live, export it back to NDJSON instead of rebuilding over it (see <a href="/docs/observability/kibana#building">Building and Exporting</a>).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="access-log">Security Access Log</h2>
  <p>Besides the JSON events, every request is written to <code>/var/log/nginx/security.log</code> with the
  routing decision attached. The format in <code>nginx.conf</code>:</p>
  <pre><code>log_format security '$remote_addr - $remote_user [$time_local] "$request" '
                   '$status $body_bytes_sent "$http_referer" '
                   '"$http_user_agent" "$http_x_forwarded_for" '
                   'session_id="$session_id" route="$route_decision" '
                   'risk_score="$risk_score" suspicious="$suspicious_activity" '
                   'request_score="$request_score" decayed_score="$decayed_score" '
                   'offenses="$offenses" decay_state="$decay_state" '
                   'uri_class="$uri_class" patterns="$threat_patterns" '
                   'reason="$route_reason" cache_status="$upstream_cache_status"';
access_log /var/log/nginx/security.log security;</code></pre>

  <h2 id="pillars">Logs, Metrics and Traces</h2>
  <p>Everything lands under the <code>honeypot-*</code> data view (<code>honeypot-&#123;log_type&#125;-%Y.%m.%d</code>),
  so all three pillars are queryable in one place.</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Pillar</th><th>Indices</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Logs</td><td><code>nginx_access</code>, <code>nginx_security</code>, <code>nginx_error</code>, <code>suricata</code>, <code>suricata_fast</code>, <code>docker</code>, <code>redis</code></td><td>Both Vector processes run with end-to-end acknowledgements, blocking (never dropping) buffers and generous retries, so backpressure holds and retries instead of silently losing a batch.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Traces</td><td><code>nginx_security</code>, <code>nginx_error</code></td><td>The per-request decision trace (<code>request_score</code>, <code>decayed_score</code>, <code>carried_score</code>, <code>offenses</code>, <code>decay_state</code>, <code>uri_class</code>, <code>patterns</code>, <code>route_reason</code>) is this stack's request trace.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Metrics</td><td><code>vector_metrics</code>, <code>host_metrics</code>, <code>pool_state</code>, <code>pool_events</code></td><td><code>vector_metrics</code> is both Vector processes' own health, told apart by <code>vector_role</code> = <code>edge_shipper</code> or <code>siem_aggregator</code>. <code>host_metrics</code> is the edge host's CPU, memory, disk and network. Both come from <code>internal_metrics</code> / <code>host_metrics</code> sources, flattened with <code>metric_to_log</code>. <code>pool_state</code> and <code>pool_events</code> come from <code>pool_manager</code>.</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>The Kibana dashboards for these are described in <a href="/docs/observability/kibana#new-dashboards">Kibana Dashboards</a>.</p>
</DocsLayout>
